var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"pluto/","page":"Examples","text":"<p>The static version (with no interactive elements) of Pluto notebook with <b>RadiationPyrometers.jl</b> usage is available at: <a href=\"../radiation_pyrometry.html\">radiation_pyrometry.html</a>\n.</p","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.jl","page":"Pyrometers","text":"Pyrometers.jl is a Julia package designed for modeling, simulating, and evaluating non-contact temperature measurements focused on preformance. It provides calculation methods for brightness (radiation) and two-color (ratio) pyrometers operating at discrete wavelengths or over integrated spectral bands.\n\nThe package is engineered for zero-allocation execution, making it optimal for real-time control loops, industrial data parsing, and automatic differentiation.","title":"Pyrometers.jl"},{"category":"type","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer","page":"Pyrometers","text":"Pyrometer type implements the following methods:\n\n[``]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer-Tuple{Any}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"(p::AbstractPyrometer)(i; T_starting::Number=1000.0)\n\nVarious versions of pyrometers calling to get the measured temperature from external radiation \n\nSurface emissivity is taken from pyrometer \n\np(i::Number) - intensity provided as a single number \n\np(i::Union{IsothermalSpectralQuantity , AbstractDiscreteQuantity}) - intensity is provided as temperature independent discrete of continuos quantity \n\nSurface emissivity is provided externally as a continuous function AbstractContinuousOrDiscreteQuantity\n\np(i::Number , ϵ::AbstractContinuousOrDiscreteQuantity) - single number intensity \n\np(i::Union{AbstractDiscreteQuantity , IsothermalSpectralQuantity} , ϵ::AbstractContinuousOrDiscreteQuantity) - continuous or discrete intensity (temperature imdependent)\n\nSurface emissivity , measured intensity (temperature dependent with temperature radiation_temperature) is provided as a continuos or discrete quantity \n\np(i::AbstractContinuousOrDiscreteQuantity,      radiation_temperature::Number,      ϵ::AbstractContinuousOrDiscreteQuantity) - continuous or discrete intensity (temperature imdependent)\n\n!Important \n\nIf the intensity is provided as a single number it should be the same quantity as the pyrometer acceps \ne.g. if `p` is `TwoBandsRatioPyrometer` the intensity should be the ratio of two integral \n(within pyrometer's working range) intensities. Example :\n```julia\n    import PlanckFunctions: band_power\n    p = TwoBandsRatioPyrometer((2.0 , 3.0), (4.0 , 5.0))\n    Ttrue = 1200.0\n    i_number = band_power(1200 , λₗ = 2.0 , λᵣ = 3.0)/band_power(1200 , λₗ = 4.0 , λᵣ = 6.0)\n    p(i_number) # returns Ttrue\n    # Pyrometers.Planck (which is PlanckFunctions)\n```\n\nOn the opposite side , for continuous intensity (including temperature dependent) \nit should be the intensity itself , the procedure of measurements \nextracts pyrometer's specific signal from this function internally\n  ```julia\n    import PlanckFunctions: band_power , ibb\n    p = TwoBandsRatioPyrometer((2.0 , 3.0) , (4.0 , 5.0))\n    Ttrue = 1200.0\n    i_iso = IsothermalSpectralQuantity(\n                    Base.Fix2(ibb , Ttrue)\n    ) # wrapper around planck spectral intensity (not spectral ratio)\n\n    p(i_iso) # returns Ttrue\n```\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer"},{"category":"type","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.BBPoint","page":"Pyrometers","text":"BBPoint type stores data on thermal emission spectrum and its  first and second derivatives it also stores \"Measurements \" vector  which further can be fitted? it also provides the constructor BBPoint(Imeasured,λ) -  Imeasured is a measured spectrum                       -  λ - wavelength vector (in μm)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.BBPoint"},{"category":"type","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.GenericDifferentiableSpectralQuantity","page":"Pyrometers","text":"GenericDifferentiableSpectralQuantity(f, backend=AutoForwardDiff())\n\nAn AD-backend agnostic wrapper for a temperature- and wavelength-dependent  spectral quantity f(λ, T).\n\nArguments\n\nf: The core function f(λ, T).\nbackend: An ADTypes.AbstractADType token specifying the preferred \n\nAD framework (e.g., AutoForwardDiff(), AutoZygote(), AutoEnzyme()).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.GenericDifferentiableSpectralQuantity"},{"category":"type","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.MWPPoint","page":"Pyrometers","text":"MWPPoint type stores data of thermal emission spectrum of a real body with\n\nemissivity polynomial approximation, and  its first and second derivatives it also stores \"measurements\" vector which further can be fitted, it also  provides the constructor MWPPoint(Imeasured,λ,initialx,polynomialtype)  where:     -  Imeasured is a measured spectrum     -  λ - wavelength vector (in μm)     - initialx - starting parameters vector (initialx[end] - starting temperature,         x[1:end-1] - emissivity approximation coefficients)     - polynomial_type - string of polynomial (this value governs the Vandermonde matrix form)\n\nN - number ob wavelength points Nx3 - 3N used to store the intermediate data for planck function and derivarives evaluation L - length of optimization variables vector P -   number of variables to be optimized (can be less or equal to the number of ) NxP - NP number of elements in jacobian  PxP - PP number of elements in hessian Pm1 - P-1 number of parameters approximating  NxPm1 - N(P-1) number of vandermatrix elements T - type of data\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.MWPPoint"},{"category":"type","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.Pyrometer","page":"Pyrometers","text":"Pyrometer{N, T} <: AbstractPyrometer{N, T}\n\nType representing a radiation (brightness) pyrometer system.\n\nSupports both single-wavelength (monochromatic) and narrow-band (integrated spectral range) instruments.\n\nFields\n\ntype::Symbol: A unique identifier or descriptive name for the pyrometer model.\nλ::SVector{N, T}: The operating wavelength(s). For single-wavelength pyrometers (N=1), it holds the target wavelength. For spectral band pyrometers (N=2), it defines the boundaries [λₗ, λᵣ].\nϵ::Base.RefValue{T}: In-place mutable effective emissivity of the measured target surface.\n\nConstructors\n\nPyrometer(type::Symbol, D::DataType=Float64)\n\nConstruct a predefined pyrometer configuration extracted from the DefaultPyrometersTypes dictionary.\n\nPyrometer(λ::NTuple{N, T}; type::Symbol=:def, ϵ::Number=1.0)\n\nConstruct a pyrometer using a tuple of wavelengths λ, which is automatically converted into a static vector SVector.\n\nPyrometer(λ::Union{AbstractVector{T}, T}; type::Symbol=:def, ϵ::Number=1.0)\n\nUniversal constructor accepting λ either as a single scalar number (monochromatic) or as a vector (band boundaries).\n\nExamples\n\n# Create a predefined pyrometer configuration from the package dictionary\np_builtin = Pyrometer(:P)\n\n# Monochromatic pyrometer at 0.65 μm\np_single = Pyrometer(0.65, ϵ=0.85)\n\n# Wide band pyrometer covering 2.4 μm to 8.5 μm\np_band = Pyrometer((2.4, 8.5), type=:mid_ir, ϵ=0.33)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.Pyrometer"},{"category":"type","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.RatioPyrometer","page":"Pyrometers","text":"RatioPyrometer{N, T, DT} <: AbstractPyrometer{N, T}\n\nType representing a ratio (two-color) pyrometer system. \n\nSupports both two-wavelength monochromatic systems and two-band ratio systems.\n\nFields\n\ntype::Symbol: A unique identifier or descriptive name for the pyrometer model.\nλ::NTuple{2, DT}: The spectral parameters. For monochromatic systems, DT <: Number (a pair of wavelengths). For band-ratio systems, DT <: Tuple (a pair of wavelength ranges (λₗ, λᵣ)).\nϵ1::Base.RefValue{T}: In-place mutable emissivity value for the first channel/band.\nϵ2::Base.RefValue{T}: In-place mutable emissivity value for the second channel/band.\n\nConstructors\n\nRatioPyrometer(λ::Union{NTuple{2, T}, NTuple{2, NTuple{2, T}}}; type::Symbol=:def, ϵ1::Number=1.0, ϵ2::Number=1.0)\n\nConstruct a RatioPyrometer object. The type parameter is automatically inferred as either monochromatic or band-based depending on the structure of λ.\n\nExamples\n\n# Two-color monochromatic pyrometer (0.85 μm and 1.05 μm)\np_mono = RatioPyrometer((0.85, 1.05), ϵ1=0.9, ϵ2=0.88)\n\n# Two-band ratio pyrometer (bands: 1.5–1.8 μm and 2.0–2.4 μm)\np_band = RatioPyrometer(((1.5, 1.8), (2.0, 2.4)), ϵ1=0.5, ϵ2=0.5)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.RatioPyrometer"},{"category":"type","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.SingleWavelengthPyrometer","page":"Pyrometers","text":"SingleWavelengthPyrometer{N, T, DT} <: AbstractPyrometer{N, T}\n\nType single -wavelengh pyrometer system. \n\nFields\n\ntype::Symbol: A unique identifier or descriptive name for the pyrometer model.\nλ::SVector{1, DT}: working wavelength ,μm\nϵ::Base.RefValue{T}: In-place mutable emissivity value.\n\nConstructors\n\nSingleWavelengthPyrometer(λ::Number; type::Symbol=:def, ϵ::Number=1.0)\n\nExamples\n\n# Single-wavelength pyrometer (0.85 μm)\np = SingleWavelengthPyrometer(0.85, ϵ=0.9)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.SingleWavelengthPyrometer"},{"category":"type","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.SpectralBandPyrometer","page":"Pyrometers","text":"SpectralBandPyrometer\n\nType alias for a brightness pyrometer over a defined spectral band.  Evaluates signals by integrating the Planck distribution function over the defined spectral range.\n\nFields\n\ntype::Symbol: A unique identifier or descriptive name for the pyrometer model.\nλ::SVector{2, DT}: Spectral range \nϵ::Base.RefValue{T}: In-place mutable emissivity value.\n\nConstructors\n\nSpectralBandPyrometer(λ1::Number , λ2::Number ; type::Symbol=:def, ϵ::Number=1.0)\nSpectralBandPyrometer( λ1::NTuple{2 , <:Number} ; type::Symbol=:def, ϵ::Number=1.0)\n\nExamples\n\n# Spectral band pyrometer for 2.0 - 4.5 μm spectral band \np_band = SpectralBandPyrometer(2.0 , 4.5 , ϵ=0.9)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.SpectralBandPyrometer"},{"category":"type","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.SpectralQuantitiesProduct","page":"Pyrometers","text":"Wrapper around two AbstractSpectralQuantity or Number product\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.SpectralQuantitiesProduct"},{"category":"type","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.TabularQuantity","page":"Pyrometers","text":"TabularQuantity{LT <: AbstractVector , ET <: AbstractVector} <: AbstractDiscreteQuantity{LT , ET }\n\nType wrapper around discrete quantity with two columns λ and i\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.TabularQuantity"},{"category":"type","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.TwoBandsRatioPyrometer","page":"Pyrometers","text":"TwoBandsRatioPyrometer\n\nTwo bands spectral ratio pyrometer, working on two wide spectral bands \n\nFields\n\ntype::Symbol: A unique identifier or descriptive name for the pyrometer model.\nλ1::NTuple{2, <:Number}: First band\nλ1::NTuple{2, <:Number}: Second band\nϵ1::Base.RefValue{T}: First band emissivity.\nϵ2::Base.RefValue{T}: Second band emissivity.\n\nConstructors\n\nTwoBandsRatioPyrometer(λ1::NTuple{2 , <:Number} , λ2::NTuple{2 , <:Number} ; type::Symbol=:def, ϵ::Number=1.0)\n\nExamples\n\n# Spectral band pyrometer for 2.0 - 4.5 μm spectral band \np_band = TwoBandsRatioPyrometer((2.0 , 4.5) , (7.0 , 9.1) , ϵ1 = 0.4 , ϵ2 = 0.93)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.TwoBandsRatioPyrometer"},{"category":"type","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.TwoWavelengthRatioPyrometer","page":"Pyrometers","text":"TwoWavelengthRatioPyrometer\n\nTwo-color spectral ratio pyrometer, working on two single wavelengths \n\nFields\n\ntype::Symbol: A unique identifier or descriptive name for the pyrometer model.\nλ1::Number: First wl\nλ1::Number: Second wl\nϵ1::Base.RefValue{T}: First wavelength emissivity.\nϵ2::Base.RefValue{T}: Second wavelength emissivity.\n\nConstructors\n\nTwoWavelengthRatioPyrometer(λ1::Number , λ2::Number ; type::Symbol=:def, ϵ1::Number=1.0 , ϵ2::Number=1.0 )\nTwoWavelengthRatioPyrometer( λ::NTuple{2 , <:Number} ; type::Symbol=:def, ϵ1::Number=1.0 , ϵ2::Number=1.0)\n\nExamples\n\n# Two-color pyrometer for 2.0 and  4.5 μm wavelength\np_band = TwoWavelengthRatioPyrometer(2.0 , 4.5 , ϵ1=0.9 , e2 = 0.5)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.TwoWavelengthRatioPyrometer"},{"category":"type","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.ViewFactorGeometry","page":"Pyrometers","text":"Type stores the geometry parameter `ξ= F * A₁/A₂` , where `F` is view factor  , \n`A₁` and `A₂` are surface under measurement area and external heater area\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.ViewFactorGeometry"},{"category":"type","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.AbstractContinuousOrDiscreteQuantity","page":"Pyrometers","text":"Common type for all continuous or discrete quantities\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.AbstractContinuousOrDiscreteQuantity"},{"category":"constant","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.DefaultPyrometersTypes","page":"Pyrometers","text":"Default pyrometers types\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.DefaultPyrometersTypes"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Base.isless-Tuple{Pyrometer, Pyrometer}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"Base.isless(p1::Pyrometer,p2::Pyrometer)\n\nVector of Pyrometer objects can be sorted using isless\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.isless"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#PlanckFunctions.eval_Dₜ-Union{Tuple{B}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{GenericDifferentiableSpectralQuantity{F, B}, Any, Any}} where {F, B}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"Planck.eval_Dₜ(::GenericDifferentiableSpectralQuantity{F, B}, _, _) where {F, B}\n\nDefault implementation of GenericDifferentiableSpectralQuantity returns an error\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PlanckFunctions.eval_Dₜ"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers._get_epsilon_equivalent-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"_get_epsilon_equivalent(p::AbstractPyrometer)\n\nReturns emissivity equivalent to be used in computations, for partial radiation pyrometers  typeof(p) <: Pyrometer returns emissivity value , for  typeof(p) <: RatioPyrometer gives e-slope value (two-bands equivalent emissivities ratio )\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers._get_epsilon_equivalent"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers._local_interpolate-Tuple{Number, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"_local_interpolate(λ_target::Number, λ_nodes::AbstractVector, ϵ_nodes::AbstractVector)\n\nSingle point interpolation  of discrete data \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers._local_interpolate"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers._simpson_even-Union{Tuple{TY}, Tuple{TX}, Tuple{AbstractVector{TX}, AbstractVector{TY}}} where {TX, TY}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"_simpson_even(x::AbstractVector{TX}, y::AbstractVector{TY}) where {TX, TY}\n\nSimpsons methods for evenly-spaced grids \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers._simpson_even"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers._simpson_uneven-Union{Tuple{TY}, Tuple{TX}, Tuple{AbstractVector{TX}, AbstractVector{TY}}} where {TX, TY}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"_simpson_uneven(x::AbstractVector{TX}, y::AbstractVector{TY}) where {TX, TY}\n\ninternal function for Simpson's integration (uneven grids)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers._simpson_uneven"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers._to_halley-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"_to_halley(tpl , i , ϵ)\n\nInternal function which converts arguments to Roots.jl Halley method from PlanckFunctions Dₜ ... function \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers._to_halley"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers._trapz-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"_trapz(x::AbstractVector, y::AbstractVector)\n\ninternal function for trapezoidal integration \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers._trapz"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.combine_effective_epsilon_derivatives-Tuple{ViewFactorGeometry, Any, Any}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"combine_effective_epsilon_derivatives(geom::ViewFactorGeometry, eo_tpl, es)\n\nConverts the derivatives of effective emissivity according to the chain rule. eo_tpl is a tuple of (value, first derivative, second derivative) of the target surface emissivity. es is fixed and does not depend on temperature (but can depend on wavelength).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.combine_effective_epsilon_derivatives"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.convert_temperature-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer, Any, Union{Number, PlanckFunctions.AbstractSpectralQuantity, Pyrometers.AbstractDiscreteQuantity}}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"convert_temperature(p::AbstractPyrometer , Tmeasured  , ϵ_new)\n\nConverts temperature Tmeasured measured using pyrometer p with it specified emissivity  to a new temperature measured with ϵ_new , the type of ϵ_new depends on the type of pyrometer  if ϵ_new is a Number than if p is RatioPyrometer it assumes e_new is e_slope, if  e_new is NTuple{2 , Number} it modifies both emissivities at two wavelength\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.convert_temperature"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.disc-Tuple{AbstractVector, Pyrometers.MWPPoint}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"disc(x::AbstractVector,bp::MWPPoint)\n\nFills emissivity, thermal emission spectrum,evaluates the residuals vector and calculates its norm for the current BandPyrometry point Input:     x - optimization variables vector, x=[a1...an,T],     where a1...an - emissivity approximations coefficients, T  - temperature      bp - (modified) current spectral band pytometry point Fills emissivity, thermal emission spectrum,evaluates the residuals vector and calculates its norm for the current BandPyrometry point Input:     x - optimization variables vector, x=[a1...an,T],     where a1...an - emissivity approximations coefficients, T  - temperature      bp - (modified) current spectral band pytometry point\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.disc"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.disc-Tuple{Any, Pyrometers.BBPoint}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"disc(T , e::BBPoint)\n\nEvaluates the least-square discrepancy between measured and calculates spectra Input:     x - optimization variables vector, x=[a1...an,T],     where a1...an - emissivity approximations coefficients, T  - temperature      e - (modified) current bb thermal emission point  Evaluates the least-square discrepancy between measured and calculates spectra Input:     x - optimization variables vector, x=[a1...an,T],     where a1...an - emissivity approximations coefficients, T  - temperature      e - (modified) current bb thermal emission point \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.disc"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.effective_emissivity-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractRadiationGeometry, Number, Number}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"effective_emissivity(geometry::AbstractRadiationGeometry, eo::Number, es::Number)\n\nreturns effective emissivity \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.effective_emissivity"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.em_cons!-Tuple{AbstractArray, AbstractVector, Pyrometers.MWPPoint}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"em_cons!(constraint_value::AbstractArray,\n                        x::AbstractVector, \n                        bp::MWPPoint)\n\nIn-place filling of two-elemnt vector of [minimum,maximum] emissivity in the whole  wavelength range   This function is used in the constraints Inputs:     constraint_value - (modified)  two-element vector to be modified in-place     x - optimization variables vector, x=[a1...an,T], where a1...an - emissivity approximations coefficients, T  - temperature      bp - (modified) \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.em_cons!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.emissivity!-Tuple{Pyrometers.MWPPoint, AbstractVector}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"emissivity!(bp::MWPPoint,x::AbstractVector)\n\nFills emissivity for the current BandPyrometry point Input:     bp - (modified) current spectral band pytometry point     x - optimization variables vector, x=[a1...an,T],     where a1...an - emissivity approximations coefficients, T  - temperature    Fills emissivity for the current BandPyrometry point Input:     bp - (modified) current spectral band pytometry point     x - optimization variables vector, x=[a1...an,T],     where a1...an - emissivity approximations coefficients, T  - temperature   \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.emissivity!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.evaluate_box_constraints-Union{Tuple{Pyrometers.MWPPoint{N, Nx3, P, NxP, PxP, Pm1, NxPm1, Pm1xPm1, T}}, Tuple{C}, Tuple{B}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Pm1xPm1}, Tuple{NxPm1}, Tuple{Pm1}, Tuple{PxP}, Tuple{NxP}, Tuple{P}, Tuple{Nx3}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{Pyrometers.MWPPoint{N, Nx3, P, NxP, PxP, Pm1, NxPm1, Pm1xPm1, T}, B}, Tuple{Pyrometers.MWPPoint{N, Nx3, P, NxP, PxP, Pm1, NxPm1, Pm1xPm1, T}, B, C}} where {N, Nx3, P, NxP, PxP, Pm1, NxPm1, Pm1xPm1, T, B<:Union{Nothing, Tuple{T, T}, Tuple{var\"#s29\", var\"#s29\"} where var\"#s29\"<:Union{NTuple{Pm1, T}, StaticArraysCore.StaticArray{Tuple{Pm1}, T, 1}}}, C<:Union{Nothing, Tuple{T, T}}}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"box_constraints(bp::MWPPoint)\n\nEvaluates box-constraint of the problem\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.evaluate_box_constraints"},{"category":"function","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.external_source_corrected_temperature","page":"Pyrometers","text":"external_source_corrected_temperature(p::Pyrometer, Tmeasured::Number, \n                Tsource::Number ,\n                ϵ_src::Union{Number , AbstractSpectralQuantity}  , \n                geometry::AbstractRadiationGeometry=EnclosureGeometry())\n\nA convenience forwarding method that extracts the baseline emissivity automatically from the pyrometer (get_emissivity(p)) and applies the universal multi-reflection stray radiation correction.\n\nArguments\n\np::Pyrometer: The target single-channel pyrometer instance.\nTmeasured::Number: The raw, uncorrected temperature reading (in Kelvin).\nTsource::Number: The temperature of the parasitic external background source (in Kelvin).\nϵ_src::Union{Number, AbstractSpectralQuantity}: The background source emissivity (constant or functional).\ngeometry::AbstractRadiationGeometry: Geometric parameter configuration layout (defaults to EnclosureGeometry()).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.external_source_corrected_temperature"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.external_source_corrected_temperature-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer, Number, Union{Number, IsothermalSpectralQuantity}, Number, Union{Number, IsothermalSpectralQuantity}, EnclosureGeometry}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"external_source_corrected_temperature(p::AbstractPyrometer, \n                            Tmeasured::Number, \n                            ϵ_surf::Union{IsothermalSpectralQuantity ,  Number},\n                            Tsource::Number , \n                            ϵ_src::Union{IsothermalSpectralQuantity ,  Number}  , \n                            ::EnclosureGeometry)\n\nComputes the true surface temperature of an object by isolating and removing parasitic reflected  radiation originating from an external heated source (e.g., furnace walls, refractory lining)  within the pyrometer's spectral operating range. ```\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.external_source_corrected_temperature"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.feval!-Tuple{Pyrometers.BBPoint, Number}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"feval!(e::BBPoint,t::Float64)\n\nEvaluates bb intensity for temperature t\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.feval!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.feval!-Tuple{Pyrometers.MWPPoint, AbstractVector}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"feval!(bp::MWPPoint,x::AbstractVector)\n\nFills both the emissivity and the thermal emission spectrum for the current BandPyrometry point\n\nInput:     bp - (modified) current spectral band pytometry point     x - optimization variables vector, x=[a1...an,T],     where a1...an - emissivity approximations coefficients, T  - temperature\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.feval!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.fill_emissivity_box_constraint!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{CN}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{Any, Any, ScaledPolynomials.VanderMatrix{N, CN, T}, Tuple{T, T}}} where {N, CN, T}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"fill_emissivity_box_constraint!(lb,ub,::VanderMatrix{N, CN, T, NxCN, CNxCN, P},\n            val_bounds::NTuple{2,T}) where {N, CN, T, NxCN, CNxCN, P<:BernsteinSymPoly}\n\nEvaluates box-boundaries for polynomial coefficients for BernsteinSymPoly  polynomial basis\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.fill_emissivity_box_constraint!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.fit_blackbody_safeguarded!-Union{Tuple{T_type}, Tuple{Nx3}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{Pyrometers.BBPoint{N, Nx3, T_type}, Number, Number, Number}} where {N, Nx3, T_type}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"fit_blackbody_safeguarded!(\nbb::BBPoint{N, Nx3, T_type}, \nT_start::Real, \nT_min::Real, \nT_max::Real\n        ) where {N, Nx3, T_type}\n\nDefault solver for BBPoint with bc using Halley method\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.fit_blackbody_safeguarded!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.fit_integral-Tuple{PlanckFunctions.AbstractSpectralQuantity, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"fit_integral(a::AbstractSpectralQuantity , l1, l2 , measured)\n\nFits the temperature of any spectral quantity to a specified value  , \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.fit_integral"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.fit_ϵ!-Union{Tuple{P}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{Vector{P}, D, Vector{T}}} where {D<:Number, T<:Number, P<:Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"fit_ϵ!(p::Vector{P} , Treal::D , Tmeasured::Vector{T}) where {D <: Number ,T <: Number , P <: AbstractPyrometer}\n\nFits the emissivity of pyrometers to make measured temperature Tmeasured fit fit the real temperature Treal\n\nInput: p - pyrometer objects vector , [Nx0] Treal - real temperature of the surface, Kelvins Tmeasured - temperatures measured by the pyrometers, in Kelvins, [Nx0]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.fit_ϵ!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.fit_ϵ-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer, Number, Number}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"fit_ϵ(p::AbstractPyrometer , Tmeasured::Number , Treal::Number)\n\nFinds the emissivity or e_slope \n\nArguments :\n\np - pyrometer object Treal - real temperature of the surface, Kelvins Tmeasured - temperature measured by the pyrometer, Kelvins\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.fit_ϵ"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.fit_ϵ_wavelength!-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer, Float64, Float64}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"fit_ϵ_wavelength!(p::Pyrometer,Tmeasured::Float64,Treal::Float64)\n\nFits emissivity and returns it as a vector of the same size as pyrometer's wavelength region Some pyrometers has 2-wavelength, other work on a single wavelength, for two-wavelength pyrometers e_out will be a two-element vector Input:     p - pyrometer object     Tmeasured - temperature measured by the pyrometer, Kelvins     Treal - real temeprature of the surface, Kelvins \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.fit_ϵ_wavelength!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.fit_ϵ_wavelength!-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{T}, D, Vector{D}}} where {T<:Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer, D<:Number}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"fit_ϵ_wavelength!(p::Vector{T},Treal::D,Tmeasured::Vector{D})   where {T<: AbstractPyrometer , D <: Number}\n\nThe same as fit_ϵ! except that it returns the vector of fitted emissivities  of the same length to the total number of wavelength in all pyrometers in vaector p, e.g. if p[i] is the narrow-band pyrometer \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.fit_ϵ_wavelength!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.fix_temperature-Tuple{PlanckFunctions.AbstractSpectralQuantity, Number}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"fix_temperature(q::AbstractSpectralQuantity , fixed_temperature::Number) -> ::IsothermalSpectralQuantity\n\nFixes AbstractSpectralQuantity temperature converting it to IsothermalSpectralQuantity\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.fix_temperature"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.full_wavelength_range-Tuple{}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"full_wavelength_range()\n\nCreates the wavelengths vector covered by default pyrometers see DefaultPyrometersTypes \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.full_wavelength_range"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.full_wavelength_range-Union{Tuple{Vector{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer","page":"Pyrometers","text":"full_wavelength_range()\n\nCreates the wavelengths vector covered by all pyrometers in vector p\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.full_wavelength_range"},{"category":"function","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.get_single_wavelength_value","page":"Pyrometers","text":"get_single_wavelength_value(l::Number, _ , e::AbstractDiscreteQuantity ) -> Number\nget_single_wavelength_value(l::NTuple{N}, _ , e::AbstractDiscreteQuantity ) -> NTuple{N}\n\nFunctions to extract single interpolated values from disctrete data \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.get_single_wavelength_value"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.grad!-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, Pyrometers.MWPPoint}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"grad!(g::AbstractVector , x::AbstractVector , bp::MWPPoint)\n\nIn-place filling of the gradient vector of MWPPoint at point x Input:     x - optimization variables vector, x=[a1...an,T],     where a1...an - emissivity approximations coefficients, T  - temperature     bp - (modified, recalculates residual vector and Jacobian if the      currently stored value was obtaibed for another optimization variables array)     current spectral band pytometry point      In-place filling of the gradient vector of MWPPoint at point x Input:     x - optimization variables vector, x=[a1...an,T],     where a1...an - emissivity approximations coefficients, T  - temperature     bp - (modified, recalculates residual vector and Jacobian if the      currently stored value was obtaibed for another optimization variables array)     current spectral band pytometry point     \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.grad!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.grad!-Tuple{AbstractVector, Number, Pyrometers.BBPoint}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"grad!(g::AbstractVector,t::Float64 ,e::BBPoint)\n\nIn-place filling of the gradient of least-square problem of bb thermal emission spectrum fitting  Input:     g - gradient vector to be filled     t  - temperature      e - (modified) current bb thermal emission ppoint \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.grad!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.hess!-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector, Pyrometers.MWPPoint}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"hess!(h , x::AbstractVector,bp::MWPPoint)\n\nIn-place filling of the whole hessian matrix for MWPPoint at point x Input:     ha - Hessian matrix to be filled in-place     x - optimization variables vector, x=[a1...an,T],     where a1...an - emissivity approximations coefficients, T  - temperature     bp - (modified) current spectral band pytometry point       In-place filling of the whole hessian matrix for MWPPoint at point x Input:     ha - Hessian matrix to be filled in-place     x - optimization variables vector, x=[a1...an,T],     where a1...an - emissivity approximations coefficients, T  - temperature     bp - (modified) current spectral band pytometry point      \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.hess!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.hess!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{M}, Tuple{Any, Number, Pyrometers.BBPoint{M, N, T}}} where {M, N, T<:Number}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"hess!(h,t::Float64,e::BBPoint)\n\nIn-place filling of least-square problem hessian matrix      Input:         h - hessian          T - temperature          e - (modified) current bb thermal emission point  \n\nIn-place filling of least-square problem hessian matrix      Input:         h - hessian          T - temperature          e - (modified) current bb thermal emission point  \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.hess!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.hess_approx!-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector, Pyrometers.MWPPoint}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"hess_approx!(ha, x::AbstractVector,bp::MWPPoint)\n\nIn-place filling of the approximate hessian (Hₐ = Jᵀ*J (J - Jacobian))  of MWPPoint at point vector x, approximate Hessian can be used  in optimization methods to approximate the full Hessian (e.g. in Gauss-Newton or Levenberg-Marquardt methods)\n\nInput:     ha - Hessian matrix to be filled in-place     x - optimization variables vector, x=[a1...an,T],     where a1...an - emissivity approximations coefficients, T  - temperature     bp - (modified) current spectral band pytometry point     \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.hess_approx!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.integral_emissivity-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer, Union{PlanckFunctions.AbstractSpectralQuantity, Pyrometers.AbstractDiscreteQuantity}, Number}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"integral_emissivity(p::AbstractPyrometer , ϵ::AbstractContinuousOrDiscreteQuantity , Tref::Number)\n\nEvaluate the integral emissivity within the pyrometer's working range using external spectral emissivity provided as discrete or continuous ϵ \n\nλ vector must be sorted in ascending order\n\nArguments\n\np - pyrometer object  ϵ - emissivity data Tref - reference temperature\n\nReturns tuple of single or two numbers depending on pyrometer's type\n\nE.g.  for SpectralBandPyrometer ϵ_int is computed as: ϵ_int = ∫ϵ(λ)ibb(λ , Tref)dλ / ∫ibb(λ , Tref)dλ\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.integral_emissivity"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.integrate-Tuple{SpectralBandPyrometer, Union{IsothermalSpectralQuantity, Pyrometers.AbstractDiscreteQuantity}}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"integrate(p::AbstractPyrometer ,  temperature:: Number , intensity::AbstractContinuousOrDiscreteQuantity) \nintegrate(p::SpectralBandPyrometer , \n            intensity::Union{AbstractDiscreteQuantity , IsothermalSpectralQuantity})\n\nConverts the input spectral intensity given as a discrete set of values for wavelengths λ to the pyrometer signal.\n\nReturns the quantity, which is equal to the type of pyrometer signal , e.g. if pyrometer is \n\nSingleWavelengthPyrometer returns the spectral intensity at the wavelength of pyrometer\nSpectralBandPyrometer  - total intensity within the spectral range the pyrometer\nTwoWavelengthRatioPyrometer  - tuple of intensities at two wavelengths \nTwoBandsRatioPyrometer  - tuple of total intensities within two bands \n\nλ , intensity  (both at the same time) if the intensity is provided as a discrete set of points \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.integrate"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.is_single_wavelength-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"is_single_wavelength(::Pyrometer{N}) where N\n\nTrue if the Pyrometer is a single wavelength or two-color\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.is_single_wavelength"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.is_spectral_band-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"is_spectral_band(p::AbstractPyrometer)\n\nTrue if p wprks in spectral band (not at single wavelength(s))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.is_spectral_band"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.jacobian!-Tuple{Pyrometers.MWPPoint, AbstractVector}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"jacobian!(bp::MWPPoint , x::AbstractVector)\n\nFills the Jacobian matrix for current bandpyrometry point Input:     x - optimization variables vector, x=[a1...an,T],     where a1...an - emissivity approximations coefficients, T  - temperature     bp - (modified, stores Jacobian internally) current spectral band pytometry point  Fills the Jacobian matrix for current bandpyrometry point Input:     x - optimization variables vector, x=[a1...an,T],     where a1...an - emissivity approximations coefficients, T  - temperature     bp - (modified, stores Jacobian internally) current spectral band pytometry point \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.jacobian!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.lm_step-Union{Tuple{P}, Tuple{Nx3}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{Any, Pyrometers.MWPPoint{N, Nx3, P}, Real}} where {N, Nx3, P}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"lm_step(x, p::MWPPoint{N, Nx3, P}, λ::Real) where {N, Nx3, P}\n\nFunction to evaluate the Levenberg-Marquardt step \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.lm_step"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.measure-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer, Number, Union{Number, Tuple{var\"#s26\", var\"#s26\"} where var\"#s26\"<:Number}}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"measure(p::AbstractPyrometer , i::D ; T_starting::T=600.0) where {D <: Number, T <: Number}\n\nCalculates the \"measured\" temperature from \"measured\" signal i. The signal units should be: W/(m²⋅sr⋅μm) - for a single wavelength pyrometer ,  W/(m²⋅sr) - for  wide - band pyrometer , dimentionless  - for sigle wavelength spectral ratio and wide-band spectral ratio; \n\nArguments:\n\np - pyrometer object i - measured signal T_starting  - temperature hint\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.measure-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer, Number, Union{PlanckFunctions.AbstractSpectralQuantity, Pyrometers.AbstractDiscreteQuantity}}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"measure(p::AbstractPyrometer  , \n                imeasured::Number , \n                ϵ::AbstractContinuousOrDiscreteQuantity; \n                T_starting::Number = 600.0)\n\nEmissivity of the surface is provided as spectral quantity but measured intencity is a number\n(somebody else integrated it)\n\nArguments\n\np: AbstractPyrometer object \nimeasured : measured signal \nϵ : surface emissivity, can be provided as \n\n(optional)\n\nT_starting : starting temperature for nonlinear eqaution solver\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.measure-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer, Union{IsothermalSpectralQuantity, Pyrometers.AbstractDiscreteQuantity}, Union{PlanckFunctions.AbstractSpectralQuantity, Pyrometers.AbstractDiscreteQuantity}}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"measure(p::AbstractPyrometer , i::Union{IsothermalSpectralQuantity , AbstractDiscreteQuantity} , \n                ϵ::AbstractContinuousOrDiscreteQuantity; \n                T_starting::Number = 600.0 , segbuf=nothing, rtol=sqrt(eps(Float64)))\n\nMeasures temperature from external radiation i provided as a spectral quantity which does'n depend on temperature, taking into account the surface emissivity ϵ\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.measure-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer, Union{IsothermalSpectralQuantity, Pyrometers.AbstractDiscreteQuantity}}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"measure(p::AbstractPyrometer , i::Union{IsothermalSpectralQuantity , AbstractDiscreteQuantity}; \n                T_starting::Number = 1000.0  , \n                segbuf=nothing, kwargs...)\n\nInput radiation is provided as temperature independent spectral quantity  , the emissivity is taken from the  pyrometer's default (thus we know it is both wavelength and temperature independent,probably integrated)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.measure-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer, Union{PlanckFunctions.AbstractSpectralQuantity, Pyrometers.AbstractDiscreteQuantity}, Number, Union{PlanckFunctions.AbstractSpectralQuantity, Pyrometers.AbstractDiscreteQuantity}}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"measure(p::AbstractPyrometer , i::Union{IsothermalSpectralQuantity , AbstractDiscreteQuantity} , \n                ϵ::AbstractContinuousOrDiscreteQuantity; \n                T_starting::Number = 600.0)\n\nMeasures temperature from external radiation i provided as a spectral quantity which depends on temperature  radiation_temperature, taking into acoount the surface emissivity ϵ , which can depend on surface temperature\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.measure-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer, Union{PlanckFunctions.AbstractSpectralQuantity, Pyrometers.AbstractDiscreteQuantity}, Number}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"measure(p::AbstractPyrometer , i::AbstractContinuousOrDiscreteQuantity,\n                    radiation_temperature::Number; segbuf=nothing, kwargs...)\n\nInput radiation intensity is provided as a temperature-dependent spectral quantity with known temperature  (it can be some sort of parameter, not temperature) \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.measure-Tuple{RatioPyrometer, Tuple{T, T} where T, Union{PlanckFunctions.AbstractSpectralQuantity, Pyrometers.AbstractDiscreteQuantity}}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"measure(p::RatioPyrometer  , \n                imeasured::NTuple{2} , \n                ϵ::AbstractContinuousOrDiscreteQuantity; \n                T_starting::Number = 600.0 ,  segbuf=nothing, rtol=sqrt(eps(Float64)))\n\nEvaluates the temperature from signal measured in two channels of two-color pyrometer \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.produce_pyrometers-Tuple{}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"produce_pyrometers()\n\nCreates the vector of all default pyrometers \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.produce_pyrometers"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.residual!-Tuple{Pyrometers.MWPPoint, AbstractVector}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"residual!(bp::MWPPoint,x::AbstractVector)\n\nFills emissivity, thermal emission spectrum and evaluates the residuals vector  for the current BandPyrometry point\n\nInput:     bp - (modified) current spectral band pytometry point     x - optimization variables vector, x=[a1...an,T],     where a1...an - emissivity approximations coefficients, T  - temperature    \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.residual!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.residual!-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{N3}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{Pyrometers.BBPoint{N, N3, T}, D}} where {N, N3, T<:Number, D<:Number}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"residual!(e::BBPoint,t::Float64)\n\nEvaluates the residual vector between calculated and measured bb thermal  emission intensity spectrum Evaluates the residual vector between calculated and measured bb thermal  emission intensity spectrum\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.residual!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.set_emissivity!-Tuple{Pyrometer, Number}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"set_emissivity!(p::Pyrometer,em_value)\n\nSetter for spectral emissivity\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.set_emissivity!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.set_emissivity!-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer, Union{PlanckFunctions.AbstractSpectralQuantity, Pyrometers.AbstractDiscreteQuantity}, Number}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"set_emissivity!(p::AbstractPyrometer,  ϵ::AbstractContinuousOrDiscreteQuantity , Tref::Number)\n\nSetting the emissivity from external data ϵ\n\nArguments\n\nλ - wavelength ,  ϵ - spectral emissivity \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.set_emissivity!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.set_emissivity!-Tuple{RatioPyrometer, Number}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"set_emissivity!(p::RatioPyrometer , em_value::Number)\n\nFor spectral ratio pyrometer  em_value is the e-slope (emissivities ratio)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.set_emissivity!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.set_emissivity!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{RatioPyrometer{N, T}, Tuple{D, D}}} where {D<:Number, N, T}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"set_emissivity!(p::RatioPyrometer{N , T} , em_value::NTuple{2 , D}) where {D <: Number , N , T}\n\nIf emissivity is provided as a tuple it is considered as two emissivities at two spectral ranges of  ratio pyrometer\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.set_emissivity!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.signal-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer, Number, Number}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"signal(p::AbstractPyrometer , Tmeasured::Number)\n\nReturns the signal value which will give the temperature Tmeasured\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.signal"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.signal-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer, Number, Union{PlanckFunctions.AbstractSpectralQuantity, Pyrometers.AbstractDiscreteQuantity}}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"signal(p::AbstractPyrometer , Tmeasured::Number, ϵ::AbstractContinuousOrDiscreteQuantity)\n\nReturns the signal value which will give the temperature Tmeasured\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.signal"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.stray_radiation_corrected_temperature-Tuple{Pyrometer, Number, Number, Number}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"stray_radiation_corrected_temperature(p::Pyrometer, Tmeasured::Number, source_intensity::Number)\n\nCorrect the measured temperature for a single-channel brightness pyrometer when the external  background radiation (source_intensity) is provided directly as an integrated signal.\n\nThe baseline surface emissivity is automatically retrieved from the pyrometer instance.  The system geometry is assumed to be an enclosure (EnclosureGeometry), meaning multiple mutual  reflections are neglected, yielding a simple effective reflectivity of r_eff = 1 - ϵ_surf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.stray_radiation_corrected_temperature"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.stray_radiation_corrected_temperature-Tuple{Pyrometer, Number, PlanckFunctions.AbstractSpectralQuantity, Union{Number, IsothermalSpectralQuantity}}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"stray_radiation_corrected_temperature(p::Pyrometer, Tmeasured::Number, \n                                                ϵ_surf::AbstractSpectralQuantity,\n                                                source_intensity::Union{Number, IsothermalSpectralQuantity})\n\nSurface emissivity can be temperature-dependent , external radiation must be provided as an isothermal quantity \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.stray_radiation_corrected_temperature"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.stray_radiation_corrected_temperature-Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer, Number, Any}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"stray_radiation_corrected_temperature(p::AbstractPyrometer, \n                                    Tmeasured::Number, \n                                    source_intensity\n                                    ) where T<:Number\n\nTakes emissivity from the pyrometer \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.stray_radiation_corrected_temperature"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.stray_radiation_corrected_temperature-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer, Number, Union{Tuple{T, T}, T}, PlanckFunctions.AbstractSpectralQuantity, Number}} where T<:Number","page":"Pyrometers","text":"stray_radiation_corrected_temperature(p::AbstractPyrometer, \n    Tmeasured::Number, \n    ϵ_surf::Union{T , NTuple{2,T}},\n    incident_radiation_function::AbstractSpectralQuantity ,  \n    radiation_temperature::Number ; \n    segbuf=nothing, rtol=sqrt(eps(Float64)) ) where {T <: Number}\n\nCorrect the measured temperature when the external background source emits a known temperature-dependent  intensity profile (which is not required to be an ideal blackbody spectrum).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.stray_radiation_corrected_temperature"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.stray_radiation_corrected_temperature-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer, Number, Union{Tuple{T, T}, T}, Union{IsothermalSpectralQuantity, Pyrometers.AbstractDiscreteQuantity}}} where T<:Number","page":"Pyrometers","text":"stray_radiation_corrected_temperature(p::AbstractPyrometer, \n                                    Tmeasured::Number, \n                                    ϵ_surf::Union{T, NTuple{2, T}} ,\n                                    source_intensity::AbstractContinuousOrDiscreteQuantity \n                                    ) where T<:Number\n\nwhen the surface emissivity is a Number external radiation can be integrated \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.stray_radiation_corrected_temperature"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.stray_radiation_corrected_temperature-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{RatioPyrometer, Number, Tuple{T, T} where T, Tuple{T, T}}} where T<:Number","page":"Pyrometers","text":"stray_radiation_corrected_temperature(p::RatioPyrometer, Tmeasured::Number, \n                                                source_intensity::NTuple{2 , T}) where T <: Number\n\nCorrect the measured temperature for a dual-channel ratio pyrometer when the external  background radiation signals for both channels are directly provided as a tuple (source_intensity).\n\nThe baseline surface emissivities for both channels are automatically retrieved from the pyrometer instance.  The system geometry is assumed to be an enclosure (EnclosureGeometry), meaning multiple mutual  reflections are neglected, yielding independent channel reflectivities r_eff = 1 - e_surf.  Returns the true temperature resolved from the corrected signal ratio.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.stray_radiation_corrected_temperature"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.subrange_view-Tuple{Number, Number, Pyrometers.AbstractDiscreteQuantity}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"subrange_view(λ1::Number , λ2::Number , e::AbstractDiscreteQuantity)\n\nThe view of subranges of discrete data within the λ1...λ2 spectral range \n\nMust return the Tuple{<:AbstractVector , <:AbstractVector} : (l  , e), where l and e are wavelength and quantity views \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.subrange_view"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.subrange_view-Tuple{Number, Number, TabularQuantity}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"subrange_view(λ1::Number , λ2::Number , λ::AbstractVector , i::AbstractVector)\n\nReturns view of two vectors based on λ[ λ1 <= λ <= λ2] , λ must be sorted in ascending order\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.subrange_view"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.subrange_view-Tuple{Tuple{T, T} where T, Tuple{T, T} where T, Pyrometers.AbstractDiscreteQuantity}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"subrange_view(λ1::NTuple{2} , λ2::NTuple{2} , e::AbstractDiscreteQuantity)\n\nThe view of subrange of discrete data within the λ1[1]...λ1[2] and λ2[1]...λ2[2] spectral range \n\nMust return the NTuple{2 , D} where Tuple{<:AbstractVector , <:AbstractVector} : ((l1 , l2)  , (e1 , e2)) ,  where l1 - e1and l2 - e2 are wavekengths and values views within the first and the second band \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.subrange_view"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.switch_the_type-Tuple{Float64}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"switch_the_type(λ::Float64)\n\nReturns the type (which can be used as a key of DefaultPyrometersTypes dict) depending on the wavelengh Input:     λ - wavelength in μm\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.switch_the_type"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.try_emissivity-Union{Tuple{ET}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{Union{Pyrometers.BBPoint{N}, Pyrometers.MWPPoint{N}}, ET}} where {N, ET<:Union{Number, StaticArraysCore.SVector{N}}}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"try_emissivity(bb::BBPoint , ϵ::Number)\n\ndivides the measured intensity by the input emissivity \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.try_emissivity"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.wavelengths_number-Tuple{}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"wavelength_number()\n\nReturns the length of wavelengths vector covered by the DefaultPyrometersTypes (all default pyrometers wavelengh region)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.wavelengths_number"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.wavelengths_number-Union{Tuple{Union{Tuple{Vararg{D}}, AbstractVector{<:D}}}, Tuple{D}} where D<:Pyrometers.AbstractPyrometer","page":"Pyrometers","text":"wavelengths_number(p::Vector{Pyrometer})\n\nReturns the total number of wavelength for the vector of pyrometers\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.wavelengths_number"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.wlength-Union{Tuple{Pyrometer{N}}, Tuple{N}} where N","page":"Pyrometers","text":"wlength(::Pyrometer{N}) where N\n\nReturns the number of wavelengths \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.wlength"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.∇!-Tuple{Pyrometers.BBPoint, Number}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"∇!(e::BBPoint , t::Number)\n\nFills the first derivative of Planck function  Input:     T  - temperature      e - (modified) current bb thermal emission point    Fills the first derivative of Planck function  Input:     T  - temperature      e - (modified) current bb thermal emission point   \n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.∇!"},{"category":"method","location":"pyrometers/#Pyrometers.∇²!-Tuple{Pyrometers.BBPoint, Number}","page":"Pyrometers","text":"∇²!(e::BBPoint , t::Number)\n\nFills the second derivative of Planck function \n\nInput:\n    T  - temperature \n    e - (modified) current bb thermal emission point\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.∇²!"},{"category":"section","location":"","page":"Pyrometers.jl","text":"Pyrometers.jl is a Julia package designed for modeling, simulating, and evaluating non-contact temperature measurements focused on preformance. It provides calculation methods for brightness (radiation) and two-color (ratio) pyrometers operating at discrete wavelengths or over integrated spectral bands.\n\nThe package is engineered for zero-allocation execution, making it optimal for real-time control loops, industrial data parsing, and automatic differentiation.","title":"Pyrometers.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Pyrometers.jl","page":"Pyrometers.jl","text":"Pyrometers.jl is a Julia package designed for modeling, simulating, and evaluating non-contact temperature measurements focused on preformance. It provides calculation methods for brightness (radiation) and two-color (ratio) pyrometers operating at discrete wavelengths or over integrated spectral bands.\n\nThe package is engineered for zero-allocation execution, making it optimal for real-time control loops, industrial data parsing, and automatic differentiation.\n\n","title":"Pyrometers.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Main-Functionality","page":"Pyrometers.jl","text":"Temperature Inversion (measure): Solves for the actual surface temperature from a given raw detector signal power handle using fast root-finding methods.\nReflection Compensation (corrected_temperature): Subtracts ambient background radiation, stray furnace reflections, and grey-body noise from raw measurements to find the reflection-free target temperature.\nSignal Generation (signal): Simulates the expected radiant power or intensity value hitting a detector for any given target temperature.\nCalibration Optimization (fit_ϵ / fit_ϵ!): Back-calculates the necessary effective emissivity or spectral ratio slope (e_slope) required to match a pyrometer's reading with a known reference temperature.","title":"Main Functionality"}]
}
